## 1. The methods, in plain words

- **Walk-forward validation.** Each month from {{ vy[0] }} to {{ vy[1] }} is forecast by models refitted on the hours before it only, less the last {{ K.embargo_days }} days (the embargo). The three models compared are fitted on the same folds: v2, v1's form on the same forecast and v1 as shipped, rebuilt in v2's harness.
- **The error.** The mean absolute percentage error (MAPE): on average, by how much the forecast misses actual demand. The signed bias says in which direction: bias = (demand − forecast) / demand, positive when the forecast is too low.
- **The uncertainty of a mean.** Neighbouring hours share the weather; so whole weeks are resampled, {{ K.n_boot|num }} times, to get a 95% interval around each mean.
- **The decision rule, fixed in the plan before any v2 replay.** On the common hours (those where demand and the three forecasts exist), v2's error is compared with each other model's, hour by hour, with a 95% interval over whole weeks. A comparison is **better** when the upper bound of its interval is below zero, **worse** when the lower bound is above it, **not separated** otherwise; the bounds are judged as printed, to a hundredth of a point. The comparisons are made again on the cold hours, those whose temperature forecast is below the {{ ordinal(100 * K.cold_quantile) }} percentile of those of {{ span(K.design_years) }} ({{ v.config.cold_c|num(1) }} °C): chosen on the input known at issue, never on the outcome. The verdict of each comparison: *better* (the comparison is better and its cold tail is not worse); *better on average, the cold tail untestable* (fewer than {{ K.min_cold_hours }} cold hours or fewer than {{ K.min_cold_weeks }} weeks); *not shown better*; *worse*. "v2 is better than v1" is said only of the verdict against v1 as shipped, and only on held-out data; "the GAM earns its place" only of the verdict against v1's form. The input's comparison (v1's form on the forecast against v1 as shipped) is reported, never judged.
- **Beside, never judged.** The {{ K.peak_hours }} busiest hours of each period judged, {{ span(vy) }} as one period, then each replayed year (by actual demand, among all the hours that have one): each model's signed error, or why it has no forecast. The coverage of v2's intervals, with the sentence on its {{ nominal|pct(0) }} (section 5).
- **The prediction interval.** Its half-width is a quantile of the relative errors |demand − forecast| / forecast. For each validation month, it is the quantile of rank ⌈(n + 1) × {{ nominal|num(2) }}⌉ of the n out-of-sample errors of the {{ K.calibration_folds }} months before, already scored when that month's model is fitted; for the frozen model, the same quantile over the errors of the {{ K.calibration_folds }} months before the replayed year, already scored when it is fitted. It is the form of a split conformal interval, without its finite-sample coverage guarantee: these hourly errors are autocorrelated and come from other models. The only evidence is the coverage measured out of sample.
- **The frozen model.** In production the model is not refitted: it is fitted once, frozen with its hash, then replayed hour by hour.
- **The thresholds.** For each season, the weekly thresholds are quantiles of v2's validation weeks: MAPE above the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.mape_high) }} percentile, bias outside the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.bias_low) }}–{{ ordinal(K.centiles.bias_high) }} percentile range, coverage below the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.coverage_low) }} percentile.
- **Drift.** Each week, the distribution of the issued temperature forecasts and that of v2's demand forecasts are compared with the same weeks (± {{ K.drift_half_window }}) of earlier years, with Evidently's normed Wasserstein distance. The alarm level is the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.drift_high) }} percentile of that distance over the validation weeks.
- **The decision table.** Each week, at most one recommendation, with its evidence: (1) a data-quality failure, a day no temperature forecast fed included → investigate the data; (2) the same failure {{ K.consecutive_weeks }} weeks in a row, broad across the week, or the bias on the same side of its band {{ K.sustained_weeks }} weeks in a row → retrain; (3) coverage below its threshold {{ K.consecutive_weeks }} weeks in a row, broad, the bias inside its band → recalibrate the intervals; (4) the same input drifting {{ K.consecutive_weeks }} weeks in a row, performance within its thresholds → investigate the data. "Broad": the week stays beyond its threshold when its {{ K.trim_days }} most extreme days are set aside. A bad demand day spoils, through the {{ K.lags[0] }} h and {{ K.lags[1] }} h lags, only itself, the next day and the same day the next week; a bad temperature forecast spoils at most two consecutive days (section 2). The monitor recommends; it never acts.

## 2. The forecast input

**The source.** The temperature is the NOAA National Blend of Models (NBM), its NBS product for station CYUL (Montréal-Trudeau, the airport of v1's primary station), as archived by the Iowa Environmental Mesonet: points every three hours, in degrees Fahrenheit, which this project interpolates to the hour and converts to degrees Celsius. The files are downloaded by script, never committed.

**The feeding rule.** A forecast is **available** {{ fin.availability_lag_h|num(0) }} hours after its issue time: this is an **assumption**, since the archive records no arrival time, and every result that depends on the forecast rests on it. For day D, among the {{ K.max_runs_back }} freshest forecasts available at D's origin (D {{ K.origin_utc }} UTC), the freshest whose own points bracket each of D's 24 hours is taken, each of those points finite and inside the data-quality bounds. Interpolation is linear in time, inside a single forecast; two forecasts are never mixed. Each training day carries the forecast issued for it by the same rule, never an observation.

**On {{ span(K.design_years) }}.** {{ fin.fed_days|num }} days fed out of {{ fin.days|num }}; {% if fin.days_not_fed %}the days no forecast fed, with their reason: {% for d in fin.days_not_fed %}{{ d.day|day }} ({{ REASON.get(d.reason, d.reason) }}){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}. Such a day has no forecast from v2 nor from v1's form on the forecast; v1 as shipped has one, and the common hours leave it out{% else %}no day was left without a forecast{% endif %}. Ranks of the forecast used (0: the freshest): {% for k, n in fin.rank_counts.items() %}rank {{ k }}, {{ n|num }} days{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}. Hour of the forecast used, by year: {% for y, hours in fin.run_hour_by_year.items() %}{{ y }}: {% for h, n in hours.items() %}{{ h }} h UTC ({{ n|num }} days){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}.

