## 10. Limitations

{% set high_thr = [] %}{% for s in ['winter', 'spring', 'summer', 'fall'] %}{% if th24.performance[s].coverage_low|round(3) > nominal|round(3) %}{% set _ = high_thr.append(s) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
- **The choice and 2024.** v2's family and input were chosen on {{ span(vy) }}; {{ r.year }} had been seen through v1. Neither proves v2 better: only a clean {{ HY }} test of record can (section 9).
- **The availability assumption.** The archive records no arrival time for the forecasts: their availability is assumed ({{ fin.availability_lag_h|num(0) }} hours after issue), and the sensitivity of section 2 measures what an availability of {{ K.late_h|num(0) }} hours instead of {{ K.lag_h|num(0) }} would cost; a longer delay is not measured. The NBM's model changes over the years without being tracked.
- **One forecast for a province.** A single forecast point stands for Québec's weather, as a single station did in v1.
- **Conditional coverage.** {% if sides.season.under or sides.decile.under or HBS.under %}v2's interval under-covers {{ groups(sides.season.under, sides.decile.under, HBS.under) }} (section 5).{% if sides.season.under %} The coverage thresholds of {{ groups(sides.season.under, []) }} are drawn from weeks that already had this under-coverage: the monitor does not flag it as a degradation.{% endif %}{% else %}No season, no hour block and no decile is under-covered in the sense of section 5.{% endif %}{% if high_thr %} Conversely, the coverage threshold of {{ groups(high_thr, []) }} ({% for s in high_thr %}{{ th24.performance[s].coverage_low|pct(1) }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}) exceeds the {{ nominal|pct(0) }} nominal: a week covered beyond nominal can be flagged low there.{% endif %}{{ '' }}
- **Extrapolation.** Below the training range, the curves are straight lines; the decision rule's cold tail and section 3's count of hours outside the range measure what that costs.
{% set MEASURED = '(measured on ' ~ p.year ~ ', the rehearsal year, and on ' ~ r.year ~ ', seen by the developers)' %}
{% if E23 is not none and E24 is not none %}
- **Episodes of several days** {{ MEASURED }}. {% if E23.worst or E24.worst or E23.recal_worst or E24.recal_worst %}An episode of several days can give a false alarm with nothing wrong in the model: {% else %}An episode is not a model failure: {% endif %}{{ episode_limit(E23, p.year, K.mild) }}; {{ episode_limit(E24, r.year, K.mild) }} (sections 7 and 8).
- **Level shift** {{ MEASURED }}. {{ level_limit(level, p.year, K.step_horizon) }} {{ level_limit(level24, r.year, K.step_horizon) }} {% set why_ = level_why([(p.year, level), (r.year, level24)]) %}{% if why_|trim %}{{ why_ }} {% endif %}The power tables measure a gap between forecast and demand, not a level shift.
- **The echo exclusion** {{ MEASURED }}. On a degraded model whose bias changes side, it suppresses "retrain" on {{ p.year }} in {{ E23.echo.suppressed|num }} trials out of {{ E23.echo.runs|num }} and delays it in {{ E23.echo.delayed|num }}, and on {{ r.year }} in {{ E24.echo.suppressed|num }} trials out of {{ E24.echo.runs|num }} and delays it in {{ E24.echo.delayed|num }}; that is the price of avoiding the false alarms of an episode followed by its echo.
{% else %}
- **False alarms and blind spots.** The diagnostics {% if E23 is none %}of {{ p.year }}{% endif %}{% if E23 is none and E24 is none %} and {% endif %}{% if E24 is none %}of {{ r.year }}{% endif %} did not finish: their measures are not shown (sections 7 and 8).
{% endif %}
{% set DY = drift_years %}
- **Drift false alarms in {{ r.year }}.** The alarm levels are the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.drift_high) }} percentile of the validation weeks of {{ span(K.threshold_years[r.year|string]) }}, whose references spanned {{ DY.temperature.calibration[0] }} to {{ DY.temperature.calibration[1] }} earlier years for the temperature forecast and {{ DY.forecast.calibration[0] }} to {{ DY.forecast.calibration[1] }} for the demand forecast; in {{ r.year }}, they span {{ DY.temperature.replay }} and {{ DY.forecast.replay }}. The drift false-alarm rate in {{ r.year }} is not measured.
- **Rule 4 and a real change in temperature.** A real, sustained drift in the temperature forecast, which the model follows, performance within its thresholds, can give "investigate the data": the rule does not tell a real change in the weather from a data error. The rule stays as it is.
- **Real-time data.** The values are those downloaded on {{ dq.sources.hydroquebec.retrieved_utc[:10] }} (demand), from {{ (dq.sources.eccc|map(attribute='first_retrieved_utc')|min)[:10] }} on (observed weather) and from {{ (dq.sources.nbm.years.values()|map(attribute='retrieved_utc')|min)[:10] }} on (NBM forecasts); no revision is modelled, and a forecast's arrival is deduced from its issue time.
- **Independence.** The model, the checks and this report come from AI agents working under the direction of a single owner, without independent human review of the code. Reviews were made by fresh-context agents and by another vendor's AI model, which is not independent validation in SR 11-7's sense.

## 11. The breaks, on synthetic data

Each gate and each monitor has a test that injects the fault it must catch; a control injects nothing and must stay quiet. The `breaks` run runs them all; the report refuses to be generated if any failed. No break reads the real data: the quality gates, the guards and the render are tested on small fabricated data, and the clock and the monitors in a synthetic world (`tests/replay_world.py`), with a drift alarm level set by hand at {{ world.drift_alarm|num(1) }}. That a break fires in that world shows the check can fire, not that it would see the same fault in real demand.

